.\" Manual page of lemongrass.
.\" This file is in the public domain.
.TH LEMONGRASS 1 2026-11-27 "lemongrass 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lemongrass \- LALR(1) parser generator with templates for C, Go and Rust
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lemongrass
.RI [ option ...]
.I grammar
.br
.B lemongrass \-\-help
.br
.B lemongrass \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lemongrass
reads the grammar file
.I grammar
and writes a parser for it.
The program is the Lemon parser generator of the SQLite project with one
change: a template file defines the syntax of the target language.
There are templates for C, Go and Rust.
With the C template the program writes the same files as the Lemon of
SQLite 3.54.1, byte for byte.
.PP
The grammar file is a Lemon grammar.
Its code blocks and its actions are in the target language.
A grammar file with code is therefore for one language.
.PP
A call names exactly one grammar file.
The program writes the parser file into the directory of the grammar file.
The parser file has the name of the grammar file with a new suffix.
The template gives the suffix:
.B .c
for C,
.B .go
for Go and
.B .rs
for Rust.
With the C template the program also writes a header file with the suffix
.BR .h .
The header file has the numbers of the tokens.
A parser in Go or Rust has these numbers as constants in the parser file.
.PP
The program also writes a report with the suffix
.BR .out .
The report lists the states of the parser and marks each conflict of the
grammar.
.PP
The program is a build tool for input that you trust.
A grammar file and a template are code.
Do not run the program on files from a source that you do not know.
.SH OPTIONS
An option and its argument are one word.
Write
.BR \-Tlempar.go ,
not
.BR "\-T lempar.go" .
.TP
.B \-b
Print only the basis of each state in the report.
.TP
.BI \-d directory
Write all output files into
.IR directory ,
which must exist.
.TP
.BI \-D name
Define the macro
.IR name .
The lines
.BR %ifdef ,
.B %ifndef
and
.B %if
of a grammar file keep or remove a part of the grammar.
They depend on the macros.
.TP
.B \-E
Print the grammar file after the program has applied the lines
.BR %ifdef ,
.B %ifndef
and
.BR %if .
The program writes no parser.
.TP
.B \-g
Print the rules of the grammar without their actions.
The rules that have an action come first.
The program writes no parser.
.TP
.B \-l
Write no line directives into the parser file.
Without this option a parser in C has
.B #line
directives and a parser in Go has
.B //line
directives.
They give the lines of the grammar file.
A parser in Rust never has line directives.
.TP
.B \-m
Write a parser file for the program
.BR makeheaders .
This option needs the C template.
.TP
.B \-p
Show in the report also the conflicts that the precedence rules resolved.
.TP
.B \-q
Write no report.
.TP
.B \-s
Print statistics of the parser on standard output.
.TP
.B \-S
Write also a file with the suffix
.BR .sql .
It has SQL statements that describe the tables of the parser.
.TP
.BI \-T file
Use
.I file
as the template.
.TP
.BI \-U name
Remove the macro
.IR name ,
which an option
.B \-D
before it has defined.
.TP
.B \-x
Print the version of Lemon and, on a second line, the version of
lemongrass.
The program reads no grammar.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Do the same as the option
.BR \-x .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the form of a call and the list of the options on standard output.
The program reads no grammar.
The message for an undefined option has the list of the original Lemon,
without the options
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version .
.PP
The program accepts the options
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-I ,
.B \-O
and
.B \-W
with any argument and ignores them.
.SH TEMPLATES
A template is the frame of the parser file.
The program copies it and puts the tables and the actions of the grammar
into it.
A template for a language other than C starts with
.B %%syntax
lines.
These lines give the syntax of the language and the suffix of the parser
file.
.PP
With the option
.B \-T
the program uses the file of that option.
Without the option it looks for a template in this order:
.IP 1. 4
A file with the name of the grammar file and the suffix
.B .lt
in place of the suffix of the grammar file.
For the grammar
.B query.y
this is
.BR query.lt .
.IP 2. 4
The file
.B lempar.c
in the current directory.
.IP 3. 4
The file
.B lempar.c
in the directory of the program, when the command names the program with
a directory.
When the command has no directory, the program looks in the directories
of the variable
.BR PATH .
.PP
Steps 2 and 3 look only for the file name
.BR lempar.c ,
and they do not look into the directory of the installed templates.
A call of the installed program therefore names the template with the
option
.BR \-T .
The options
.B \-E
and
.B \-g
need no template.
.PP
A template with
.B %%syntax
lines has limits.
Destructors, the directives
.BR %realloc ,
.B %free
and
.BR %stack_size_limit ,
and the option
.B \-m
work only with the C template.
The templates for Go and Rust also have no error symbol and no
.B %extra_context
directive.
.SH FILES
The command
.B make install
of the source puts the templates into the directory
.B share/lemongrass
below its prefix.
A package usually has them in these places:
.TP
.I /usr/share/lemongrass/lempar.c
The template for C.
It is the original template of Lemon.
.TP
.I /usr/share/lemongrass/lempar.go
The template for Go.
.TP
.I /usr/share/lemongrass/lempar.rs
The template for Rust.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program found no error, and the grammar has no conflict.
.TP
.B 1
The program found an error, or the grammar has a conflict.
With a conflict the program still writes the parser file and the report.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write the C parser
.BR query.c ,
the header file
.B query.h
and the report
.BR query.out :
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
lemongrass \-T/usr/share/lemongrass/lempar.c query.y
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write the Go parser
.B gen/query.go
without line directives, and write no report:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
lemongrass \-q \-l \-dgen \-T/usr/share/lemongrass/lempar.go query.y
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the rules of a grammar in a sorted list:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
lemongrass \-g query.y | grep \(aq::=\(aq | sort
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the versions:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
lemongrass \-x
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
The source of lemongrass has these documents:
.TP
.I lemon.html
The grammar language.
This is the original documentation of Lemon.
It does not describe the templates for Go and Rust.
.TP
.I go-and-rust.md
A guide for people who write a grammar for Go or Rust.
.TP
.I template-syntax.md
A guide for people who write a template for another language.
.PP
The source is at https://github.com/mge1512/lemongrass
